{"id": "1220545c55", "title": "Validate the heavy-use profiling test on the MacBook", "goal": "On the MacBook, with a fresh reservation, validate commit <id> on branch fm/task-<id>: native startup and clock ownership, enabled collectors, diagnostic CPU/heap/recording output and calibration, with reprovisioned public assets.", "userWords": "(filed from a task's report: \"Use commit <id> for the separate MacBook validation task\")\n\n\nAnswer shape: this task's deliverable is an answer, not a change, and it lands as one card. The card opens with the answer in a sentence and then draws it - how the thing works, how the options differ, what the state or the layout is - with a figure for each part of the explanation. Names, numbers, quotes and caveats are prose beside the figure they qualify; the evidence a reader who digs would want, including paths and commands, goes in <details>. When your findings warrant a concrete next move, identify it and put it in the card's actions with one recommended default; do not invent an action when the answer is a pure fact, which stays a note. Your report stays the short factual account; the card is where the answer is shown. Your report is also the answer this task keeps, and work built on it starts from it: when the captain's replies change the answer, report it again in full - the final agreed version, with his tweaks and anything decided on the card - before he chooses. Rigor: none - this task ships nothing; the card is the deliverable. Your worktree is a laboratory: install, run, and edit there freely to find the answer, but commit nothing, push nothing, and open no pull request. Nothing in it ships; it is discarded when this task closes, and anything worth keeping - paths, commands, a reproduction, a sketch of a fix - goes in the card. The card and your report are the only things that survive this task.\n\nknowledge for infinitary (candidates matched from this task's title and goal; read each with the knowledge tool before relyin