{"id": "3e85f1e400", "title": "Assess moving Infinitary's heavy CI from PRs to main", "goal": "Flesh out and assess the captain's proposal for Infinitary: no expensive CI jobs on pull requests, local agent testing unchanged, a continuous full CI run on main with fix-forward, and releases only from commits marked all green. Measure whether it would really give a big productivity boost against today's PR CI time and waits, and name the problems and the mitigations they need. Weigh the in-flight CI speed work (a task's pieces, a task, the single Mac mini virtual Mac) as context.", "userWords": "i have a bold idea and i want a fable crewmate to investigate - CI has become such a massive bottleneck for my development of infinitary here. at the current volume of changes, repeated CI runs seems like the biggest bottleneck. i wonder whether we can instead: 1) don't run any expensive CI jobs (journeys, phone checks etc) at all for each PR; 2) agents still test their changes with relevant unit tests, journeys, live validations etc locally - no change here; 3) on main branch, we have a continuous full CI run always happening. if any failures were found, we fix forward. 4) we somehow mark which commits are \"all green\", and releases can only happen from a green commit, not latest main. i want the crewmate to flesh out this concept and assess whether it has real merit. whether it will indeed give me a massive boost of productivity, what problems exist that may need mitigations", "way": "asked", "month": "2026-10", "tags": ["ci", "idea"]}
